 The center of the second circle is $(2s,s)$. There are three usual Pythagorean relations involving $2s$, $s$, $(s+r)$, and $x^2+y^2$.

  \begin{align*} 2s^2+s^2&=(s+r-x)^2+(y-s)^2\\ s^2+(s+r)^2&=(x-s)^2+y^2\\ (s+r)^2+(s+r)^2&=x^2+y^2 \end{align*}

 Subtract the second from the third and then twice the first:

